AI Tool / Version
ChatGPT – OpenAI, GPT-5.6 Sol

How AI Was Used:

I used ChatGPT during the assignment as a discussion, review, debugging and testing assistant.

For the Petri-net planning, I used AI to discuss how the track sections could be represented using Free and Occupied places, 
how train movements could be represented as transitions, and where collision or deadlock risks existed. 
This included discussion of the passenger/freight crossover, passenger priority, and the bidirectional freight routes. 

AI also helped me evaluate the idea of using separate reservation resources for the Workshop Route (Sections 3–4) and the 
Main Freight Route (Sections 3–7–11). For InterlockingImpl.java, AI was used to discuss and review specific implementation sections. 
In addTrain(), this included duplicate train names and occupied entry sections. 
In isValidRoute(), AI helped check the permitted entry/destination combinations against the railway layout.
The largest amount of discussion concerned moveTrains(), including how to prevent two trains entering the same section, validate 
requested train names before making state changes, stop the same train moving twice in one call and preserve passenger priority at the crossover.

For testing, AI suggested additional JUnit scenarios that I then ran against the implementation. These included duplicate names,
null and unknown train inputs, multiple trains competing for the same section and cleanup after failed operations. 
The test suite was expanded incrementally to 51 tests.

For debugging, I used ChatGPT to examine specific lines of my Java implementation and explain why certain behaviours were occurring. For example, AI helped me review:
Set<String> requestedTrains = new LinkedHashSet<>(); and requestedTrains.add(trainName);
when ensuring the same train could not move twice if its name appeared multiple times in moveTrains().
It also helped me verify the validation condition
if (train == null || train.currentSection == -1)
so invalid or already-exited trains were detected before any state changes occurred.

UPDATE: I later used ChatGPT to help debug the implementation using Gradescope feedback. This included identifying issues with freight route handling, 
simultaneous section-vacating movements, movement dependencies, and conflicts where multiple trains attempted to enter the same section. 
I then updated the implementation and expanded my JUnit suite with regression tests based on the failing scenarios, 
increasing the hidden test result from 66/175 to 164/175 and finally to 175/175

Verification and Review:
I reviewed the implementation independently against the supplied Interlocking interface, the assignment railway requirements 
and my Petri-net model throughout development. Changes were compiled and tested locally before being retained.
For final verification I used JDK 11.0.32 and successfully compiled the implementation using: javac InterlockingImpl.java
I also ran the JUnit 4 test suite with all 66 tests passing, checked that no .class files were tracked in Git, 
and confirmed that the repository compiled successfully using the Java version specified by the assignment.